\description{
Every result of the package that holds something to draw has a \code{plot()}
method, on base graphics only. No argument beyond the object is needed;
every method takes the same customisation: \code{main}, \code{col} or \code{pal}, and
\code{...} passed to the base-graphics call, with the session look set by
\code{\link[=plotopt]{plotopt()}}. Each method returns, invisibly, the data it drew (a data
frame, or a list of them), so the same picture can be drawn with any
other tool. Series that differ in colour also differ in line type or
point shape, and shaded cells carry their numbers, so no plot depends on
colour alone.
}
\details{
What each method draws:
\itemize{
\item Estimates with intervals, as a dot-and-interval (forest) chart:
\code{cntreg} (rate ratios, log scale), \code{dastd}, \code{grprr}, \code{sirex}, \code{svymx},
\code{mndla}, \code{thrtst} (thresholds), \code{sarml} and \code{spcure} (coefficients),
\code{mtrbnd} (bounds by treatment), \code{pbbnd} (bounds of published cells),
\code{dkfig} (bounds on the dark figure), \code{didfe} (with its Goodman-Bacon
comparisons and leave-one-out estimates when computed), \code{smdbal}
(covariate balance), \code{jmono} (rates by judge), \code{relchk} (the readings
of a table against the claim) and \code{dissix} (segregation indices).
\item Effects by time since adoption: \code{evtst} and \code{stgdid}, before adoption
in open circles and after in filled ones.
\item Paths over time: \code{synthc} and \code{sdidx} (treated against its synthetic
comparison), \code{trndrr} (series and fitted trend), \code{stkflw} (population,
person-days, average daily population and length of stay), \code{dktflw}
(clearance rate and pending cases), \code{jpipe}, \code{hsper}, \code{yoypx} (percent
change with its interval), \code{cumrsk}, \code{agemix} and \code{apcie}.
\item Curves: \code{kmrec} and \code{lftab} (survival), \code{lnroc} (lineup ROC curves),
\code{lnkpr} (the ROC of the linkage model), \code{cconc} (Lorenz curve),
\code{crossk} (cross K function), \code{bandx} (the statistic against the top
band's cap), \code{jdgiv} (sensitivity of the instrument), \code{xcser}
(cross-correlations) and \code{hwkres} (time-rescaling residuals).
\item Bars: \code{arwt}, \code{crsev}, \code{gmixlr} and \code{pedlr} (log10 likelihood ratio
by locus), \code{hypq} (workload of each unit), \code{lnsize}, \code{lostay},
\code{mmshr} (stacked, hatched), \code{pinpl} and \code{rptvic} (observed against
expected).
\item Matrices and surfaces: \code{ctabx} (residuals), \code{knxnr} (Knox ratios),
\code{qdrtst}, \code{trnmat}, \code{zcomp}, \code{kdsurf}, \code{prohs} (hot cells outlined)
and \code{sdbsim}.
\item Points: \code{bufas} (distances to sites), \code{cmpsc} (segment positions),
\code{ebshr} (raw against shrunk values), \code{ibench} (rate against
benchmark) and \code{kscan} (clusters as circles).
\item Indigenous health and wellbeing, each group's level with its interval
and a star on an estimate the source flags: \code{indgap} and \code{trchlthgap}
(ratio to the reference group, log scale), \code{dualcont} (wellness beside
distress), \code{welprof} and \code{mhcont} (every indicator, by group),
\code{healchg} (change between two periods), \code{holbal} (level and balance
of the domains), \code{commidx}, \code{stdprev}, \code{ordcmp}, and \code{cnscwb} and
\code{cwbscr} (the four components of a Community Well-Being score and the
score).
}
}
\examples{
d <- expand.grid(year = 2015:2024, town = LETTERS[1:12])
i <- match(d$town, LETTERS)
first <- rep(c(2019, 2021, NA), each = 4)
d$first <- first[i]
d$bylaw <- as.integer(!is.na(d$first) & d$year >= d$first)
d$rate <- 30 + i + 0.5 * (d$year - 2015) - 4 * d$bylaw + sin(seq_len(120))
plot(evtst(d$rate, d$town, d$year, d$first, leads = 3, lags = 3))
plot(didfe(d$rate, d$town, d$year, d$bylaw, bacon = TRUE, loo = TRUE))
one <- d[d$town \%in\% c("A", "I", "J", "K", "L"), ]
plot(synthc(one$rate, one$town, one$year, one$first))
plot(grprr(c(a = 120, b = 95, c = 30), c(a = 6e4, b = 2.1e4, c = 1.5e4)))
plot(kmrec(c(3, 8, 8, 12, 20, 24), event = c(1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1),
           grp = c("a", "a", "a", "b", "b", "b")))
plot(trnmat(c("N", "N", "E", "N", "W", "W", "E", "E"),
            c(1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3), time = c(1, 2, 3, 1, 2, 3, 1, 2)))
set.seed(1)
plot(cconc(rpois(200, 2), nsim = 0))
}
